{"name":"DNP (2,4-dinitrophenol)","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/DNP_Photo_Printer_Station.jpg/960px-DNP_Photo_Printer_Station.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DNP_Photo_Printer_Station.jpg","caption":"DNP Photo Printer Station","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["DNP is an industrial chemical sold as a diet drug in the 1930s and withdrawn after reports of cataracts, blindness and deaths. It has resurfaced online, where bodybuilders and others on looksmaxxing forums describe using it for very fast fat loss and an extremely lean look.","DNP acts as a protonophore: it lets protons leak across the inner mitochondrial membrane, so energy that would have made ATP is released as heat and metabolic rate rises. Users on forums report large weight losses over a few weeks, with heat, sweating and fever. Evidence on its effects in humans comes mainly from 1930s clinical reports, toxicology and poison-centre case data."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["Forum users describe multi-week courses during cuts, often combined with other substances and with dieting. Regulators and clinicians describe a narrow gap between effective and toxic exposure."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Documented harms include dangerously high body temperature, cataracts, nerve damage and death, with no specific antidote; overdose causes fatal hyperthermia. It was judged too toxic for human consumption under the 1938 US Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act, is banned for human consumption in many countries, has been prohibited by WADA since 2015, and was reclassified as a regulated poison in the UK in 2023."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/DNP_Photo_Printer_Station.jpg/960px-DNP_Photo_Printer_Station.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campai